[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I validated end-to-end connectivity between the two reported hosts. From PC15 (172.18.51.15) to PC16 (172.18.51.16) and back, ICMP echoes succeed bidirectionally with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond to low‑millisecond RTTs. A TCP throughput test (iperf) from PC15 to PC16 sustained ~89 Mbit/s with zero retransmissions. This proves Layer 2 and Layer 3 between the two desks are healthy. Both PCs reside in the same subnet (172.18.51.0/24) on switch10: PC15 on port fa1 and PC16 on port fa2; the switch fabric and those access ports are behaving correctly.

Mechanism: If a switchport/VLAN/duplex issue existed near PC16, we would see packet loss, ARP failures, or severe throughput degradation. Instead, clean pings and high TCP throughput confirm no such fault.

Localization: No faults detected on switch10 (ports connecting pc15 and pc16) or the attached paths toward mswitch2. The condition is not reproducible from the network side.

Root cause: No current network malfunction; the originally reported “nothing goes through” could have been transient or application/host-layer specific (e.g., file-sharing service or host firewall), but this is not evidenced now.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. Ask users to retry. If file sharing still fails despite network reachability, verify their sharing service is running and listening, and check OS firewalls on both PCs to allow the relevant ports/protocols. Capture traffic on the hosts if application issues persist.
=== END ===